# .aidocs/coding-standards.aidocs - Global Coding Standards

Baseline
- Prefer existing patterns and reuse over new abstractions.
- KISS: keep it stupidly simple.

Abstraction discipline
- The purpose of abstracting is not to be vague, but to create a new semantic level in which one can be absolutely precise.

Data-driven design
- Do not hardcode business rules or tenant/customer-specific fields in general/shared code.
- Prefer generalized domain entities/config (for example tenant/org/workspace plus role/level definitions) as data.
- If you find yourself adding special-case fields or branches for one tenant/customer, issue STOP.

Frontend and styling context
- When non-Tailwind classes appear in a class string, identify any CSS styling connected to that element before changing the markup or related styles.
- Check the relevant stylesheet, component-scoped style, or custom selector definitions that influence that element.

Reuse over reinvent
- Prefer reusing existing project interfaces (CLI/server APIs/tools) over adding new parallel implementations.

Documentation expectations
- Projects should keep backend and frontend documentation concise and actionable.
- When documentation and code disagree, issue STOP before changing either.

Data
- Prefer database designs that follow normal forms unless there is a clear, documented reason to denormalize.

Project portability
- Avoid absolute machine-specific paths in project files.

Code comments
- Comments must explain WHY, not WHAT. The code shows what — the comment adds context the code cannot.
- Do not restate code: `# Get the user` above `user = get_user()` wastes tokens and attention.
- Do not use vague qualifiers: "just", "simply", "basically", "obviously", "make sure", "remember to".
- Acceptable: tradeoffs, constraints, non-obvious behavior, cross-file dependencies, platform workarounds, security implications.
- TODO/FIXME must include what needs to change and why, not just the tag.
- Config comments should explain what changes when the value is toggled.

